     "content_html": "<p>Sample Space:</p>\n<table class=\"unnumbered\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A heart</td>\n<td>A spade</td>\n<td>A club</td>\n<td>A diamond</td>\n<td>K spade</td>\n<td>K club</td>\n<td>Q heart</td>\n<td>Q spade</td>\n<td>J heart</td>\n<td>J spade</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Jacks: Jack heart, Jack spade.</p>\n<p>Hearts: Ace hearts, Queen hearts, Jack hearts.</p>\n<p>Jack and Heart: J heart.</p>\n<p>These are not mutually exclusive.</p>\n<p>There are 10 cards.</p>\n<p>You can use the Inclusion/Exclusion Principle. If <span data-math=\"E\"></span> and <span data-math=\"F\"></span> are not mutually exclusive, then</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"{\\text{P}}\\left( {E\\,{\\text{or}}\\,F} \\right){\\text{ }} = {\\text{ }}P\\left( E \\right){\\text{ }} + {\\text{ }}P\\left( F \\right){\\text{ }}-{\\text{ P}}\"></span>(<span data-math=\"E\"></span> and <span data-math=\"F\"></span>).</p>\n<p>P(Jack or heart) = P(J) + P(heart) – P(J and heart) = <span data-math=\"\\frac{2}{{10}} + \\frac{3}{{10}} - \\frac{1}{{10}} = \\frac{4}{{10}} = \\frac{2}{5}\"></span>.</p>\n"
